# Track Clearance report (Development Issues #87): close runs, two tracks
# side by side within 5 degrees of parallel and closer than the clearance
# (13 ft, 1.79 in in HO, by default) over at least 1 in.
# Plain straights:
# - (a) 1 and 2, parallel 1.4 in apart over 20 in: listed;
# - (b) 3 and 4, yard tracks 1.5 in apart over 18 in: listed;
# - (c) 5 and 6, 2 in apart: clear, not listed;
# - (d) 8 crosses 7 at 45 degrees: close, but not side by side;
# - (e) 9 and 10 end to end, 1 in apart: in line, not side by side;
# - (f) 12 runs 0.8 in alongside 11: shorter than the 1 in minimum;
# - (g) 14, 18 in above 13 (an upper deck) and 0.05 in beside it in plan:
#   at another height, so not listed.
# Peco Code 83 #6 sidings, laid by the MCP's track geometry:
# - main 20-22 with a siding (23-25) run out to 1.4 in from it: listed
#   (the siding's straight against the main beyond the turnout);
# - main 26-28 with a siding (29-31) at 2 in: clear; near its frog the
#   diverging track is close but at the frog angle, so not a run.
# At 12 ft (1.65 in) the three runs at 1.4/1.5 in are listed; at 15 ft
# (2.07 in) the 2 in pairs are listed too. Rows at one spot share a place:
# the 1.4 in siding's curve and straight are one place, so 4 rows make 3
# places. Then Refresh marks changes: deleting track 2 gives 3 rows and
# "1 fixed"; undoing the delete gives 4 rows again and "1 new". The reports
# saved at each step are compared with trackclearance-expected/. REDRAW
# resolves the tracks' connections (macro.c), which the report reads.
